Follow-up to mlir_aie_examples_npu.log (getting_started designs). User asked to try the
vision/ and ml/ programming_examples next -- these are the categories most relevant to
this project's own theme (CNN inference primitives), as opposed to getting_started's
generic microbenchmarks.

Survey of programming_examples/vision/ and programming_examples/ml/ against this
hardware (Phoenix, ryzen_ai_npu1 in mlir-aie's own REQUIRES tags):

  - vision/{color_detect,color_threshold,edge_detect,vision_passthrough}: all support
    npu1 (`run_makefile.lit`), but every one drives the actual NPU dispatch from a C++
    host program (test.cpp, linking OpenCV) built via `make -f Makefile`. This machine
    has no `make` on PATH (checked: `which make` / `where make` both fail) and the
    mlir-aie-iron ironenv has no Python cv2 either (these need the C++ OpenCV libs
    test.cpp links against, a separate thing). Not run this pass -- would need a make
    tool (e.g. via MSYS2) and an OpenCV C++ dev package added to the machine, out of
    scope for "try the examples that already run."
  - ml/{bottleneck,conv2d,conv2d_14x14}: also Makefile-built (same `make` gap), and
    additionally require `torch` (not installed in the mlir-aie-iron env). Not run.
  - ml/norm (rms/layer via Makefile) and ml/rope: Makefile-built, `ryzen_ai_npu2`
    (Strix) only in their REQUIRES tags -- wrong chip generation for this machine
    regardless of the make gap.
  - ml/{cast_f32_bf16,dwconv1d,mm_activation_epilogue}, ml/norm's own `run_strix.lit`
    python path, and every design under ml/block_datatypes/: all tagged
    `ryzen_ai_npu2` only (Strix/AIE2P) -- do not apply to this Phoenix/AIE2 machine at
    all, not a local setup gap.
  - ml/magika: has its own `run_phoenix.lit` but is a multi-group text-classifier model
    (`group0.py`/`group2.py`, `data/`, `kernels/`) well beyond a single design file;
    left for a dedicated pass rather than folding into this one.
  - ml/mobilenet: an end-to-end mobilenet dataflow (`run_e2e.lit`, `run_strix_makefile.lit`),
    same story as magika -- substantial enough to warrant its own log, not run here.
  - ml/{eltwise,eltwise_unary,scale_shift,softmax,swiglu}: pure-Python `@iron.jit`
    designs, `ryzen_ai_npu1` + peano only in REQUIRES, no Makefile/OpenCV/torch. These
    five ran.

Cleared ~/.npu/cache before this round (fresh compiles, not cache hits). Same
mlir-aie-iron env / C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie clone (tag v1.4.2) as the prior two logs.

--- ml/eltwise/eltwise.py (bf16 vector elementwise, per-worker split across the array) ---

  python eltwise.py -d npu -o add   -> PASS!
  python eltwise.py -d npu -o mul   -> PASS!

Cache: eltwise_add_bf16_vector.cc (xclbin 17,240 B), eltwise_mul_bf16_vector.cc
(xclbin 17,304 B) -- confirms add and mul compiled to genuinely distinct kernels, not
one path reused.

--- ml/eltwise_unary/eltwise_unary.py (bf16 activation functions) ---

  python eltwise_unary.py -d npu -o relu  -> PASS!
  python eltwise_unary.py -d npu -o silu  -> PASS!
  python eltwise_unary.py -d npu -o gelu  -> PASS!

Cache: bf16_relu.cc (xclbin 23,672 B), silu_bf16.cc (xclbin 34,168 B), gelu_bf16.cc
(xclbin 35,192 B). Directly relevant to this project's own domain -- relu/silu/gelu are
exactly the activation kernels YOLOv8/ResNet-family models use, here run as standalone
hand-written AIE kernels rather than as part of a quantized ONNX graph.

--- ml/scale_shift/scale_shift.py (time-multiplexed D = A*B+C, two-phase runtime param) ---

  python scale_shift.py -d npu  -> PASS!

Two cores compute D = A*B (phase 1, `rtp = 1`), then D = D + C (phase 2, `rtp = 0`) over
the same workers, synchronized via a `WorkerRuntimeBarrier` so the runtime-parameter
buffer update is visible before each phase starts -- a different structural pattern
(same cores, two passes) than the split-and-parallelize style of the other designs here.
Cache: eltwise_mul_add_bf16_vector.cc (xclbin 13,976 B).

--- ml/softmax/softmax.py ---

  python softmax.py -d npu  -> PASS!

Cache: softmax_bf16.cc, largest xclbin of this batch (71,037 B).

--- ml/swiglu/swiglu.py ---

  python swiglu.py -d npu  -> PASS!

Cache: swiglu_bf16.cc (xclbin 21,400 B).

--- Conclusion ---

Eight more designs PASS on this machine's actual XDNA1 (Phoenix) NPU via mlir-aie/Peano,
all from a cold compile cache, all distinct kernels confirmed via the cache directories
(8 total, no collisions). This round specifically exercises ml/-category primitives
(elementwise binary ops, activation functions, a two-phase runtime-parameterized design,
softmax, swiglu) rather than getting_started's generic microbenchmarks -- closer to the
actual operator vocabulary of the CNN/detection models this repo's own pipelines quantize
and run, though still via a hand-written-kernel toolchain entirely separate from
Quark/VitisAI EP. vision/ and the remaining ml/ Makefile-built examples are not blocked
by chip generation (several are npu1-tagged) but by two missing pieces on this machine:
a `make` tool and an OpenCV C++ dev package -- a real, stated gap, not attempted to work
around this pass. ml/magika and ml/mobilenet are pure-Python npu1-capable but are
multi-file models substantial enough to warrant their own dedicated log.

No new machine state beyond the two prior mlir-aie logs.

Machine: Desktop 2 / Phoenix.
